{"cuisine/cuisine-of-niger":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-niger","n":"Niger cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Niger","intro":[["The cuisine of Niger draws on traditional African cuisines. Various spices are used and meals include grilled meat, seasonal vegetables, salads, and various sauces. Meals in Niger usually start with colorful salads made from seasonal vegetables. Moringa leaves are frequently used in salads."],["Typical Nigerien meals consist of a starch (rice being the most common) paired with a sauce or stew. The starches eaten most often are millet and rice. Staple foods include millet, rice, cassava, sorghum, maize and beans. Couscous is saved for special occasions. Porridge, wheat dumplings, and beignets are some of Niger's most common snacks. One especially well-known food is jollof rice."],["Plant agriculture production in Niger is significantly reliant upon rainfall to provide water for plants, and droughts have adversely affected Niger's agriculture production in the past, threatening the country's domestic food supply."],["Tea is a common beverage in Niger."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-niger"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Niger"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/guangxi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/guangxi-cuisine","n":"Guangxi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Guangxi cuisine is the cuisine of Guangxi, China. This cuisine ranges from Guangdong-like cuisine to Huang cuisine. The cuisine is fairly spicy. There is a difference between the city and the countryside cuisine."],["Guangxi cuisine can be divided into two styles, north and south:"],["Southern Guangxi cuisine is very similar to Cantonese cuisine. This form of Guangxi cuisine is mild and uses more seafood than meat. Example foods include Bama roasted pork, white cut chicken, and lemon duck."],["Northern Guangxi cuisine is more related to Hunan and Guizhou cuisine. It is known for its spicy, oilier, and sour tastes, with chili peppers as a versatile ingredient. Example foods include beer fish, luosifen, and Guilin rice noodles. Noodles are more commonly used in this form of cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"guangxi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guangxi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ed/%E6%A1%82%E6%9E%97%E7%B1%B3%E7%B2%89%28Guilin_Rice_Noodle_%29.jpg/500px-%E6%A1%82%E6%9E%97%E7%B1%B3%E7%B2%89%28Guilin_Rice_Noodle_%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Flickr user gigijin","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%A1%82%E6%9E%97%E7%B1%B3%E7%B2%89(Guilin_Rice_Noodle_).jpg"}},"cuisine/hyderabadi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hyderabadi-cuisine","n":"Hyderabadi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Telugu cuisine","cuisine/telugu-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Hyderabadi cuisine, also known as Deccani cuisine, is the cooking style characteristic of the city of Hyderabad and its surrounding area in Telangana, India."],["Hyderabadi cuisine is an amalgamation of Indian, Mughalai, Turkic, and Arabic also influenced by the culinary habits of common people in the Golconda Sultanate. Hyderabadi cuisine comprises a broad repertoire of rice, wheat, and meat dishes and the skilled use of various spices, herbs and natural edibles."],["The haute cuisine of Hyderabad began to develop after the foundation of the Bahmani Sultanate, and the Qutb Shahi dynasty centered in the city of Hyderabad promoted the native cuisine along with their own. Hyderabadi cuisine had become a princely legacy of the Nizams of Hyderabad as it began to further develop under their patronage."],["Hyderabadi cuisine has different recipes for different events, and hence is categorized accordingly, from banquet food, to weddings and parties, festival foods, and travel foods. The category to which the recipe belongs itself speaks of different things like the time required to prepare the food, the shelf life of the prepared item, etc."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hyderabadi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyderabadi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Roat","dish/roat"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Dassana's Veg Recipes (Punjabi)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vegrecipesofindia.com","note":"Vegetarian Hyderabadi section of Dassana Amit's Indian recipe blog.","url":"https://www.vegrecipesofindia.com/recipes/hyderabadi-cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Jewels of the Nizam: Recipes from the Khansamas of Hyderabad","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Geeta Devi","note":"Royal Hyderabadi recipes collected from the cooks of noble households, published by Rupa.","url":"https://rupapublications.co.in/book/the-jewels-of-nizam-recipes-from-the-khansamas-of-hyderabad","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Khoa","ingredient/khoa"],["Refined flour","ingredient/refined-flour"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Telugu cuisine","cuisine/telugu-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/53/Dum_ka_roat.jpg/500px-Dum_ka_roat.jpg","w":500,"h":750,"by":"Jyothivalaboju","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dum_ka_roat.jpg","of":"Roat"}},"dish/bondiola-sandwich":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bondiola-sandwich","n":"Bondiola sandwich","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Argentine cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Bondiola sandwich or bondipan is a sandwich made with thick slices of pork shoulder. They are commonly sold by street food vendors and restaurants in Argentina. The meat consists of slices of roasted or cured pork shoulder and it is usually served on crispy brioche bread. It can be topped with a variety of condiments and vegetables."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bondiola-sandwich"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bondiola_sandwich"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Brioche","ingredient/brioche"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Familia Kitchen: Argentina","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"familiakitchen.com","note":"Argentine recipes and stories on a Latino family cooking site.","url":"https://familiakitchen.com/destination/argentina/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Argentine cuisine","links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Argentine pizza","dish/argentine-pizza"],["Asado","dish/asado"],["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Fugazza","dish/fugazza"],["Jesuita (sandwich)","dish/jesuita-sandwich"],["Jésuite","dish/jesuite"],["Matambre","dish/matambre"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"],["Rogel (cake)","dish/rogel-cake"],["Salsa golf","dish/salsa-golf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0e/Bondiola_Sandwich_with_fixings.jpg/500px-Bondiola_Sandwich_with_fixings.jpg","w":500,"h":439,"by":"travelwayoflife","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bondiola_Sandwich_with_fixings.jpg"}},"dish/chahan-dish":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chahan-dish","n":"Chahan (dish)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chahan, also known as yakimeshi (Japanese: 焼飯, 焼き飯), is a Japanese fried rice dish prepared with rice as a primary ingredient and many optional additional ingredients and seasonings. The dish is typically fried, and can be cooked in a wok. Chahan may have originated in the 1860s from Chinese immigrants arriving at the port of Kobe. Chahan is a staple food in homes in Japan. Some restaurants outside Japan serve the dish as a part of their fare."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chahan-dish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chahan_(dish)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese Chinese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Sudachi Recipes: Wafu Pasta","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sudachirecipes.com","note":"Chuka recipes such as gyoza, ebi mayo and tantanmen from Japanese cook Yuto Omura.","url":"https://sudachirecipes.com/chuka-ryori-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's collection of chuka dishes as cooked in Japanese homes.","url":"https://www.justonecookbook.com/japanese-chinese-food-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"No Recipes: Japanese Chinese","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"norecipes.com","note":"Japanese Chinese recipe category on Marc Matsumoto's food site.","url":"https://norecipes.com/recipe/cuisine/japanese-chinese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese Chinese cuisine","links":[["Narutomaki","dish/narutomaki"],["Ramen shop","dish/ramen-shop"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d6/%E3%83%81%E3%83%A3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%8F%E3%83%B3.jpg/500px-%E3%83%81%E3%83%A3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%8F%E3%83%B3.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"かう","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E3%83%81%E3%83%A3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%8F%E3%83%B3.jpg"}},"dish/churchkhela":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/churchkhela","n":"Churchkhela","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Georgian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Churchkhela is a traditional Georgian candle-shaped confection. The main ingredients of churchkhela are grape concentrate, nuts, and flour. The traditional technology of churchkhela in the Kakheti region was inscribed on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Georgia list in 2015."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-churchkhela"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churchkhela"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Grape juice","ingredient/grape-juice"],["Must","ingredient/must"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Georgian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"georgianrecipes.net","note":"Recipe site dedicated to traditional Georgian dishes.","url":"https://georgianrecipes.net/about-georgian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Georgian cuisine","links":[["Achma","dish/achma"],["Ajika","dish/ajika"],["Akhasheni","dish/akhasheni"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"],["Chakhokhbili","dish/chakhokhbili"],["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"],["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"],["Dambalkhacho","dish/dambalkhacho"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"],["Khachapuri","dish/khachapuri"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khvanchkara","dish/khvanchkara"],["Kindzmarauli","dish/kindzmarauli"],["Kubdari","dish/kubdari"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"],["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"],["Mujuretuli","dish/mujuretuli"],["Nadugi","dish/nadugi"],["Nigvziani badrijani","dish/nigvziani-badrijani"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"],["Tsolikouri","dish/tsolikouri"],["Tvishi (wine)","dish/tvishi-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/23/Churchkhela.jpg/500px-Churchkhela.jpg","w":500,"h":746,"by":"Nino Ozbetelashvili from Georgia","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Churchkhela.jpg"}},"dish/gilda-snack":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/gilda-snack","n":"Gilda (snack)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Basque cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A gilda is a type of skewered tapa or appetizer from Basque Country. It combines olives, chili peppers and a preserved fish, typically an anchovy, held together by a toothpick and pickled in vinegar. By skewering the ingredients, this dish is a pincho/pintxo, and it is considered the most famous skewer of its kind. It is commonly served in bars and traverns. Today, canning and pickling companies commonly distribute them pre-made."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-gilda-snack"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilda_(snack)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["European anchovy","ingredient/european-anchovy"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]},{"h":"More from Basque cuisine","links":[["Basque cheesecake","dish/basque-cheesecake"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Goxua","dish/goxua"],["Intxaursaltsa","dish/intxaursaltsa"],["Pantxineta","dish/pantxineta"],["Pincho","dish/pincho"],["Piperade","dish/piperade"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Sukalki","dish/sukalki"],["Tuna pot","dish/tuna-pot"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c7/Gildas_en_Donostia.jpg/500px-Gildas_en_Donostia.jpg","w":500,"h":345,"by":"Biskuit from Atlanta, GA","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gildas_en_Donostia.jpg"}},"dish/jipang-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jipang-food","n":"Jipang (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Jipang is a traditional snack made out of rice or glutinous rice. This traditional snack is also sometimes called bipang or berondong beras."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jipang-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jipang_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","di